// Add lines like this to spawn processes at startup.
// Note that running niri as a session supports xdg-desktop-autostart,
// which may be more convenient to use.
// See the binds section below for more spawn examples.

// Noctalia is the bar/shell (replaces waybar). Install: sudo dnf install noctalia
// Started by noctalia.service instead of spawn-at-startup, so systemd can
// restart it when the OOM killer takes it out. The unit is WantedBy=niri.service,
// so it stays niri-only and never starts under Plasma.
// spawn-at-startup "noctalia"
// waybar disabled on purpose — Noctalia provides the bar. Do NOT run both.
// spawn-at-startup "waybar"

// NetworkManager secret agent. The wifi connections use psk-flags=1, meaning the
// PSK lives in gnome-keyring rather than the .nmconnection file, and NM will not
// connect until something offers to hand it over. Under GNOME that something is
// gnome-shell; niri has no equivalent, which is why wifi stopped persisting after
// the move. nm-applet registers the agent and also gives a tray menu.
//
// This only covers post-login. Nothing is connected at the greeter, because the
// keyring is locked until you log in.
//
// Started by nm-applet.service, not spawn-at-startup, for the same reason as
// noctalia: if the agent dies, wifi stops working with no visible cause, and
// spawn-at-startup gives no restart and no status to check.
// spawn-at-startup "nm-applet" "--indicator"

// To run a shell command (with variables, pipes, etc.), use spawn-sh-at-startup:
// spawn-sh-at-startup "qs -c ~/source/qs/MyAwesomeShell"
